# contrary_reason: in P1 (S1) and in C/A2 (S2) "contrary to reason" means the same thing: opposed to what reasoning about our perceptions establishes. The two objections differ in their grounds, not in what the phrase means.
# A1 links "sensible qualities" to "intelligible qualities". Here both name the primary and secondary qualities of matter, so A1 joins two claims, each in its own sense. It is not a bridge.
